          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.091,
            0.438,
            0.116
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.2 Halving a Square"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.136,
            0.7,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "？ Now suppose we are given a square, and we want to construct a square whose area is half that of the original square. How would you do it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.198,
            0.7,
            0.254
          ],
          "content": "One way to do it is to reverse the construction of the previous section. We draw a tilted smaller square inside the larger one:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.718,
            0.14,
            0.848,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.436,
            0.258,
            0.563,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.367,
            0.805,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "Why is the smaller inside square half the area of the larger square? Again, adding some east-west and north-south lines can explain it:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.434,
            0.416,
            0.564,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.529,
            0.468,
            0.549
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Halving a Square Using Paper"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.563,
            0.845,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": "？ Cut out a square from a piece of paper. Now make a square whose area is half the area of the first square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.609,
            0.845,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "？ Will the square having half the sidelength have half the area? Why not? How many such squares will fill the original square?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.653,
            0.846,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "Fold the square paper inward, as shown, such that the crease lines pass through the midpoints of the sides. PQRS is the required square with half the area."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.21,
